.TH H264\-SORT 1 "2026-10-27" "h264-sort" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
h264\-sort \- move H.264/AVC videos into an h.264 subdirectory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B h264\-sort
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIDIR\fR|\fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B h264\-sort
examines the video files in the given directories and moves every file whose
first video stream is H.264/AVC into a subdirectory of the same directory,
named
.B h.264
by default. Files with any other codec (HEVC, AV1, MPEG\-2, ...) are left where
they are.
.P
The codec is determined with
.BR ffprobe (1)
from the first video stream, not from the file name or extension, so a
.B .mkv
that actually contains H.264 is detected correctly and a mislabelled container
is not mistaken for H.264.
.P
Every file is probed and the answer is cached
.B per file
in
.BR ~/.cache/h264\-sort/files.tsv ,
keyed by absolute path and invalidated when the file's size or mtime changes.
A rerun over an unchanged folder therefore probes nothing, and a new download
costs exactly one probe. An earlier version cached per capture model, on the
assumption that a model always uses the same codec; that assumption was false
(re\-encodes happen part\-way through a catalogue), so it both moved HEVC files
into
.B h.264
and left H.264 files behind.
.P
Because the cache is keyed by file rather than by name, two folders are
independent: a model that is H.264 in one place and HEVC in another is handled
correctly. Entries for files that no longer exist are dropped when the cache is
next rewritten, and a moved file is re\-keyed to its new path.
.P
Files are moved, never copied, and an existing destination file is never
overwritten: a numeric suffix
.RI ( name.1.ext )
is used instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-codec " CODEC"
Collect files whose first video stream is
.I CODEC
as reported by
.BR ffprobe (1)
(default:
.BR h264 ).
The cache records whatever codec a file actually has, so switching
.B \-c
never costs a new probe for an unchanged file.
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-target " NAME"
Use
.I NAME
as the destination subdirectory instead of
.BR h.264 .
The name defaults to
.B h.264
when the codec is
.BR h264 ,
and to the codec name otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Also search subdirectories. Each directory that contains matches gets its own
target subdirectory; the target directories themselves are pruned and never
searched.
.TP
.BI \-j ", " \-\-jobs " N"
Probe
.I N
files concurrently (default: 8). Only matters for files that are not already
cached. Lower it for a flaky network mount, raise it for a local disk.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-refresh
Probe every file in this run again, ignoring the cache, and update it. Cached
files outside this run are kept.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be moved without changing anything on disk. Probe results are
still cached.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Candidate files
Only these extensions are considered, case\-insensitively:
.BR mkv ,
.BR mp4 ,
.BR mov ,
.BR m4v ,
.BR avi ,
.BR ts ,
.BR mts ,
.BR m2ts ,
.BR webm ,
.BR flv ,
.BR wmv ,
.BR mpg ,
.BR mpeg ,
.BR ogv ,
.BR 3gp .
Without
.B \-r
only the top level of each directory is scanned.
.SS Detection
Missing or changed files are run through
.BR ffprobe (1)
with
.BR \-select_streams " v:0"
and
.BR \-show_entries " stream=codec_name" .
Files with no video stream and files ffprobe cannot read are reported as
unreadable, counted, and left untouched; so are files whose codec does not
match.
.P
Probing finishes before the first move, so interrupting the scan with
.B Ctrl\-C
leaves the directory exactly as it was; files probed before the interrupt are
still written to the cache.
.SS Requirements
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.cache/h264\-sort/files.tsv
Per\-file codec cache, tab\-separated:
.IR path ", " size ", " mtime ", " codec .
Honours
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME .
Deleting it is always safe; the next run re\-probes everything.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview what would be moved in a media folder:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-n /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Move the H.264 files, leaving HEVC where it is:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Handle a folder tree, including nested captures:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-r /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.P
Re\-probe everything, for example after replacing files in place:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-R \-n /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Gather HEVC files instead, into a folder named
.BR hevc :
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-c hevc \-t hevc \-r /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No move failed. Skipped and unreadable files do not count as failures.
.TP
.B 1
At least one move failed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage (no directory or file given, invalid
.BR \-j ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ts2mkv (1),
.BR ffmpeg\-encode (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
